This distinction must be preserved in future publications and technical documentation.

### 5. Physical and software architecture

The project is intended to support different laboratory configurations rather than one fixed hardware platform.

#### 5.1 Physical subsystems

| Subsystem            | Candidate implementation                     | Main validation requirement              |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Fluidics             | Syringe/peristaltic pumps, valves            | Dispensing accuracy, contamination       |
| Motion               | Cartesian stages, robotic manipulators       | Positioning repeatability                |
| Reaction environment | Batch, flow or microfluidic reactor          | Chemical compatibility and stability     |
| Thermal control      | Heaters, Peltier devices, RTDs/thermocouples | Uniformity, dynamic response             |
| Optical sensing      | UV–Vis/NIR, LED/photodiode systems           | Calibration and signal-to-noise          |
| Molecular sensing    | Raman and external analytical instruments    | Sensitivity and spectral reproducibility |
| Embedded control     | Microcontrollers and SBCs                    | Deterministic execution                  |
| Computational layer  | Python-based orchestration and ML            | Reliability and provenance               |

A hybrid approach is preferred: open-source and locally manufacturable hardware where practical, with commercial analytical instruments where their performance justifies the cost.

#### 5.2 Asynchronous operation

The hardware and computational loops have different timescales.

- Embedded thermal control: potentially sub-second.
- Sensor acquisition: seconds.
- Chemical-state inference: after acquisition or continuously where appropriate.
- Experiment selection: after sufficient new information becomes available.
- Campaign-level planning: at a higher timescale.

A synchronous architecture would unnecessarily block faster components while slower operations finish.

The recommended design is asynchronous orchestration around a shared experimental state.

#### 5.3 Data provenance

Every experiment should preserve:

- Experiment identifier and timestamp.
- Commanded experimental conditions.
- Realized process conditions.
- Reagent identifiers and concentrations.
- Sensor calibration status.
- Instrument configuration.
- Raw analytical data.
- Inferred state and uncertainty.
- Algorithm and model versions.
- Acquisition scores and decision rationale.
- Quality-control flags.
- Failures and recovery actions.

This is a mandatory scientific-reproducibility requirement, not an optional logging feature.

#### 5.4 Failure recovery

The proposed recovery hierarchy is:

\\[ \mathrm{Retry} \rightarrow\mathrm{Recalibrate} \rightarrow\mathrm{Remeasure} \rightarrow\mathrm{Replan} \rightarrow\mathrm{Human\ intervention} \\]

Safety shutdown and emergency intervention must remain independent of the experiment planner.

